# The Grain — Chapter One: One Person There
# Linear dramatic cut. Interaction choices will be added only after the scene
# and mystery support meaningful alternatives.


label office_closing:
    stage calder_office_late_afternoon

    "At five thirty-seven, Henry Calder turns the card in the frosted-glass door from OPEN to CLOSED. It has said CLOSED for most of the afternoon. Turning it is less an announcement than a ritual."

    show ruth_ellery composed at center

    "Ruth Ellery knocks directly beneath his painted name."

    henry_calder "It's open."

    ruth_ellery "It says closed."

    henry_calder "Then why did you knock?"

    ruth_ellery "I remembered you were a liar."

    "Henry opens the door. Ruth looks first at him, then at the room: two client chairs, one filing cabinet, a telephone, a fan moving warm air from one side of the office to the other."

    ruth_ellery "Your name has gotten smaller."

    henry_calder "The door was replaced."

    ruth_ellery "I remember the door."

    henry_calder "Then my name got smaller."

    "She takes the chair that does not face the window. Henry notices and sits behind neither chair nor desk. He rests against the filing cabinet."

    henry_calder "Coffee?"

    ruth_ellery "At this hour?"

    henry_calder "It's the same coffee as this morning."

    ruth_ellery "That wasn't my concern."

    henry_calder "Good."

    "He pours one cup. Ruth accepts it despite herself, smells it, and sets it on the floor."

    henry_calder "You came all this way to insult the office."

    ruth_ellery "No. I need you for dinner."

    henry_calder "That's not how people usually phrase it."

    ruth_ellery "I'm out of practice."

    henry_calder "With dinner?"

    ruth_ellery "With needing you."

    "Henry waits. Ruth had prepared for a question. His silence makes her discard the answer."

    ruth_ellery "Tolland's. The Winter Room. Eight o'clock."

    henry_calder "The department store?"

    ruth_ellery "After closing."

    henry_calder "Is it a robbery?"

    ruth_ellery "It's a goodbye."

    henry_calder "Whose?"

    ruth_ellery "Lydia Shaw is leaving Los Angeles tomorrow morning."

    henry_calder "For where?"

    ruth_ellery "She says Portland."

    henry_calder "You don't believe her."

    ruth_ellery "I believe she's leaving. We disagree about what that means."

    henry_calder "What does she do?"

    ruth_ellery "She alters things."

    henry_calder "Clothes?"

    ruth_ellery "Mostly people. Clothes when they're watching."

    "Henry drinks the office coffee. It has declined during the conversation."

    henry_calder "Who invited me?"

    ruth_ellery "I did."

    henry_calder "Who invited you to invite me?"

    ruth_ellery "Edwin said I could bring one person."

    henry_calder "Who is Edwin?"

    ruth_ellery "He runs the Winter Room. He believes seven is an accusation and eight is a dinner party."

    henry_calder "Did you tell him what I do?"

    ruth_ellery "I told him you listen."

    henry_calder "That's not what I do."

    ruth_ellery "It's why you get paid."

    henry_calder "This is a job."

    ruth_ellery "No."

    henry_calder "You came to my office."

    ruth_ellery "You never gave me your home address."

    henry_calder "You never asked."

    ruth_ellery exposed "I am asking you to dinner, Henry. Don't make both requests humiliating."

    "It is the first time she has used his given name. He lets the fact pass without pretending not to notice it."

    henry_calder "Why me?"

    ruth_ellery composed "I need one person there who will not perform for them."

    henry_calder "People perform for me."

    ruth_ellery "Not twice."

    henry_calder "Who is 'them'?"

    ruth_ellery "People who love Lydia in incompatible ways."

    henry_calder "And you?"

    ruth_ellery "I'm compatible with everyone until they ask."

    henry_calder "What do you think will happen?"

    ruth_ellery "A woman will leave Los Angeles."

    henry_calder "That happens every day."

    ruth_ellery "Not when six people have built their lives around her staying."

    "Henry sets his coffee down beside hers. The two cups look like evidence from a meeting neither intends to admit occurred."

    henry_calder "What are you afraid of?"

    ruth_ellery exposed "She intends to forgive me."

    henry_calder "For what?"

    ruth_ellery "She hasn't told me."

    henry_calder "Have you apologized?"

    ruth_ellery "That's what makes it unpleasant."

    henry_calder "Decline."

    ruth_ellery "If I don't go, she'll forgive me in writing."

    "A traffic signal changes outside. Red light passes across the frosted glass, then is gone."

    henry_calder "Last time we were in a room together, you told the truth and hated me for hearing it."

    ruth_ellery "I hated everyone else for making it necessary."

    henry_calder "I was included."

    ruth_ellery composed "You usually are. You just stand far enough away to make it look accidental."

    "Henry takes his coat from the hook."

    henry_calder "Is there food at this dinner?"

    ruth_ellery "Edwin would consider the question actionable."

    henry_calder "I'll change my shirt."

    ruth_ellery "That is the closest thing to friendship you've ever said to me."

    henry_calder "Don't make me reconsider it."

    "Ruth picks up both coffee cups and carries them to the sink."

    jump tollands_arrival


label tollands_arrival:
    stage tollands_motor_court_after_closing
    show ruth_ellery composed at right

    "Tolland's motor court was designed so a customer could enter the store without touching the city. After closing, the dark porte cochere does the opposite. Each footstep returns from the stone."

    "The display windows are still illuminated. Inside them, mannequins attend dinners, descend painted staircases, and wait beside luggage that has never travelled. Beyond the glass, the real sales floor is dark."

    "Ruth presses a bell beside an unmarked service door."

    henry_calder "You know where the servants enter."

    ruth_ellery "Everybody who works for a living knows where the servants enter."

    show edwin_price composed at left

    "The door opens before Henry can answer. Edwin Price is narrow, silver-haired, and dressed with the severity of a man whose dining room has survived several owners."

    edwin_price "You're early."

    ruth_ellery "By thirteen minutes."

    edwin_price "For a departure, that is considered aggression."

    ruth_ellery "Edwin Price, Henry Calder."

    edwin_price "Mr. Calder. I had understood you were a friend."

    henry_calder "So had I."

    ruth_ellery "We're trying it out."

    "Edwin considers Henry's coat, shoes, hands, and expression. It is not an inspection. It is seating preparation."

    edwin_price "Miss Shaw has moved your place card twice."

    henry_calder "I didn't know I had one."

    edwin_price "Neither did she. This has not prevented her."

    stage tollands_cosmetics_floor_dark

    "Edwin leads them through the unlit cosmetics floor. In the dark, mirrors return fragments rather than people. Ruth knows the route and does not look at the displays. Henry does."

    "Near the elevators, one window is unfinished. Six mannequins face an empty seventh place beneath a white paper moon. A seamstress's chalk and a pair of scissors have been left on the floor inside the glass."

    henry_calder "Is that Lydia's work?"

    ruth_ellery exposed "No."

    "She answers too quickly to make the answer useful."

    stage tollands_service_elevator
    show ruth_ellery composed at right
    show edwin_price composed at left

    "The passenger elevators are dark. Edwin unlocks a service lift."

    edwin_price "The dining room staff left at six. I have retained one cook and one waiter, both of whom have been promised ignorance."

    henry_calder "Can they deliver?"

    edwin_price "They are professionals."

    "The service lift rises through the store. Each floor appears briefly through the wire-glass panel: furniture arranged into rooms without walls, women's dresses hanging in patient rows, toys under canvas, lamps glowing alone because no one found the correct switch."

    "Ruth watches the floor numbers. Edwin watches Ruth."

    edwin_price wary "She hasn't told me why she asked you."

    ruth_ellery "She hasn't told me either."

    edwin_price "You misunderstand. Lydia has told me three reasons. She hasn't told me which one she plans to remember."

    jump winter_room


label winter_room:
    stage winter_room_night
    show ruth_ellery composed at right
    show edwin_price composed at left
    show lydia_shaw composed at center

    "The Winter Room curves along the top floor beneath a shallow glass roof. At lunchtime it seats a hundred and forty. Tonight a single round table occupies its center, laid for eight beneath low amber lamps. Los Angeles continues beyond the windows as a field of lights without an audience."

    "Lydia Shaw sits alone, writing place cards in green ink. She is dressed simply, but nothing about the dress appears accidental. One unopened suitcase stands beside the service bar."

    "She sees Ruth, then Henry. She finishes the name she is writing before she rises."

    lydia_shaw "You brought a stranger."

    ruth_ellery "You told me not to come alone."

    lydia_shaw "Those are not the same kindness."

    ruth_ellery "Lydia Shaw, Henry Calder."

    "Lydia offers Henry her hand. Her fingers carry two shallow pin marks and a stain of green ink."

    lydia_shaw "What did she tell you about me?"

    henry_calder "That you're leaving."

    lydia_shaw "Then she told you the only thing no one here believes."

    henry_calder "Do you?"

    "Lydia smiles, not because the question is clever, but because Henry has returned it without decorating it."

    lydia_shaw "Sit beside me, Mr. Calder."

    ruth_ellery exposed "He came with me."

    lydia_shaw "That is why I would like to make it inconvenient."

    "An elevator bell sounds somewhere below. The empty store carries it upward, one clean note at a time."

    show lydia_shaw watchful at center

    "Lydia looks toward the entrance. For the first time, her composure does not fail; it becomes work."

    lydia_shaw "That will be the first person who thinks this dinner is about them."

    end next_guest_arrived
